Why I care
As a researcher spending much of my time thinking about AI security and security-adjacent topics, I often find myself so focused on theoretical problems that I forget they’re tied to actual human lives. I think it’s important to remember that the majority of the human population doesn’t live in the mental headspace that many AI/ML researchers seem to occupy nowadays, where their biggest concern is the concept of a future evil superintelligent being bringing mass irreversible job displacement, societal upheaval and disillusionment, and eventually human demise. For the average human, their concerns are about things that directly impact their present-day lives: having clean drinking water, affordable power bills, not dying in wars, etc. However, AI development is already shaping these things in ways that most may not realize. I believe those of us who understand this technology are responsible for ensuring things go well in the real world, which means we can’t spend all day stuck in our heads worrying about an unpredictable future. AI risk is not just a research problem anymore, or even a domestic or national security issue. It’s now about international safety standards, warfare, and humanity at large. As such, I hope that even the most technically oriented people in AI security and safety nowadays can explain—and more importantly, care about—how their work advances something concrete that the world needs.

For some more context, my background is mainly in technical AI/cybersecurity research. However, I’ve also done some governance and technical work with implications in AI safety and security policy at the state and national levels. The process of actually doing and reflecting upon these types of work has shaped many of my current views. Particularly, I used to think that maximizing my impact on the world meant devoting my soul to technical research, but I don’t think that’s true anymore. Throughout history, the government has intervened and shaped the course of technological development time and time again, and AI won’t be the exception. As such, I feel obligated to divert some of my attention to understanding where my work fits into the policy and national security landscape, if not engaging with or even directly participating in it.

Note that I bring up a lot of my own concerns and perspectives below, but these are all complex issues and proposing solutions to them is out of my scope. I'm currently just an undergrad with essentially no influence over how any of this plays out. Until I'm in a position to give meaningful input, I hope the people who do have both the technical background and influence will push for decisions driven not by the competitive and profit-maximizing interests of a small group of actors, but rather by what's best for a safe and democratic world.



Defining red lines: government vs. model providers
When OAI signed its agreement with the DoW back in February, many questions were left unanswered. Particularly, I found it contradictory that OAI claims it controls model safety at every step of the development and deployment process, yet is still letting the government determine the final use cases. While OAI complies with the government’s decisions on paper, I don’t see what’s stopping them from getting around that by using their own safety guardrails to restrict model usage. After all, whether or not a use case is valid is inherently dependent on what OAI's internal safety mechanisms (e.g. classifiers, testing, MTS in the loop) will allow. This brings up a broader question: if a provider’s model refuses to respond to certain prompts, then doesn’t that make the provider the one implicitly defining the boundary between lawful and unlawful use? I don’t see why the government was able to come to this agreement with OAI when its whole controversy with Anthropic was for the exact same reason: a model provider with the power to define red lines. Regardless of exactly what went down, though, I think this has highlighted the need to more clearly define the balance of decision-making power between model providers and the government. As AI is used more often in national security contexts, we'll likely run into cases where the government clashes with safety guardrails regarding the scope of “lawful” activities the model can carry out. In this case, do we loosen its guardrails so the government can use it more permissively? If we do, and the model does something unlawful that causes mass harm, then who’s responsible: the provider for not doing good enough safety training, or the government for asking for looser guardrails in the first place? These are hard questions that we will need to consider before we actually encounter such situations.
We need better-specified frontier model regulations
I also think there should be a more legitimate, standardized, and transparent basis upon which the government can regulate models and how. I bring this up in the context of the staged rollout of frontier models, where the federal government is currently granting and taking away access to frontier models on a customer by customer basis. The main questions here are: which models qualify for government control, and how far is the government allowed to go in assessing and limiting access to such models?
What can be regulated?
The first question was briefly addressed in the AI executive order, which calls for a “classified benchmarking process” to define the capability threshold for a “covered frontier model.” Working on one of these benchmarks as an intern this summer has provided me some degree of visibility into the process, which got me thinking a lot about potential challenges. Government agencies can push the labs all they want to deliver these benchmarks as quickly as possible, but that doesn’t change the fact that it takes time, effort, and experience to develop good benchmarks—all resources that we aren’t particularly abundant in. What we need is a systematic pipeline for model evaluation, but with everyone scrambling and the government itself still in the process of figuring out exactly what it needs, I’m worried that the initial benchmarking process will not be as high-quality, comprehensive, and usable as it needs to be. Moreover, when we develop open source benchmarks in industry/academia, the worst case scenario is that there’s some glaring issue with it (e.g. data leakage, poor verification schemes or test cases, under or overcoverage of certain capabilities, etc) that the community usually picks up on fairly quickly. These will probably be harder to catch in classified benchmarks, where fewer evaluations occur and fewer people will be involved in probing them from every angle to reveal issues.
How do we regulate?
The second question seems even harder to address. The government keeps calling for certifications, licensing, etc without defining what it should look like, which can create unevenness and potential loopholes in practice. At least for 41 USC 4713 (the law mitigating supply chain risks, which remains in effect by the DC Circuit’s ruling in Anthropic v. US DoW), it seems that the agencies have very little idea what to do with DoW contractors using Anthropic’s products due to issues like confusing certification language, ambiguous information flow from the DoW to enforcement bodies to contractors to subcontractors, etc. On another note, with such a lack of structure and transparency, how can we trust that the government is restricting access purely on the basis of model safety/security/capability without any ulterior motives? Theoretically, there’s nothing stopping the government from restricting model access due to political rivalries, economic concerns, etc and wrapping it up as a “classified reason” that they won’t ever have to justify to the general public. Of course, internal oversight has been built in to prevent these types of situations, but how many times in history have we seen these oversight mechanisms fail?
The information gap
Finally, these frontier regulations will further widen the gap between select “elites”—government, frontier labs, major tech and cybersecurity companies, an extremely small population of influential AI executives and researchers—and everyone else. This problem has extended beyond the technical domain and now affects democracy and the entire US population. There already exist sharp ideological divides regarding AI risk, and when I talk to my friends outside tech-related fields, I’m shocked to find out some of them have never considered or even heard of things like AGI and existential risk. However, I also can’t blame them because with all these access and information restrictions, most people significantly underestimate what the most advanced AI models are actually capable of right now. What we should be trying to do is reconcile this gap and help people see the bigger picture, not further obfuscate things.
The international landscape
Over this past year, the US government has cracked down a lot on domestic policy to ensure it has the unequivocal final say in national security decisions. We’ve seen the example of Anthropic being designated a supply chain risk, which seems less about the technicalities of their safety restrictions (which is actually lawful, in the sense that many private entities have imposed on government usage of their products in a similar manner in the past) and more about the government fundamentally opposing the concept of a private entity that can openly challenge their decisions in critical national security contexts. Many of the new AI directives are further driving this point home: for instance, NPSM 11 explicitly requires the use of “contractual clauses or other means” to ensure that “no commercial entity or adversary possesses the capability to prevent use of, disable or degrade, or materially modify without Federal Government knowledge and approval, an AI system that our men and women depend on for their missions.” I understand why the government feels threatened, but I don’t see why they’re making such a big deal out of asserting their dominance over domestic entities when they could instead be investing that time and energy into defending against foreign entities who pose far more of an actual threat to national security.
Distillation and access controls
For instance, one thing the US could look more into is better access controls for the technology, platforms, and communities that currently make mass API requests possible. The issue isn't just suspicious activity across individual accounts anymore; it’s the dispersion of traffic across API key black markets, transfer stations, proxies, etc that enable large-scale malicious activities like distillation. The way it works is that cheap API access (e.g. via black markets, reselling unused quotas, etc) allows transfer stations to provide bulk API access for many customers. These transfer stations then provide an entire pipeline for users to make malicious requests, from handling fraudulent credentials to load balancing and ultimately sending out requests across many IP addresses via proxies.

This makes distillation much harder to defend against compared to most other attacks (e.g. weight stealing) where we at least have a known target that we can guard by physically segregating or restricting access to it. Trying to detect distillation is essentially just trying to detect suspicious prompts, which is hard because attackers can use various strategies to bypass detection. For instance, espionage and other critical attacks can be carried out via a combination of jailbreaking and breaking down requests into many seemingly harmless requests. Maybe detecting suspicious CoTs or outputs is a more promising strategy, but even then, the sprawled-out proxy IP addresses make any input/output monitoring difficult since detection would require knowing which IP addresses to look at in aggregation. Moreover, the ambiguity of natural language and rapid evolution of natural language-based attacks means that there will always be cases that slip past us. This is why illegal API access and distillation are fundamentally access control issues. It would probably be most useful to explore how to develop advanced KYC, behavioral biometrics, and other identity-related methods that would ideally also be robust against things like forged identities and deepfakes.
Openness and accessibility
One of the biggest debates right now is about open-weight models vs. frontier models. I won’t go into much depth because new policies, discussions, etc are unfolding as I write this (in July 2026) so I know my opinions will continue to evolve. However, I do have some high-level opinions and ideas that are not as likely to change.

First, I believe the behind-the-scenes risks of open-weight models pose far more of a threat than open access to frontier models due to the lack of traceability. If attackers successfully carry out an attack using a frontier model, we at least retain bits and pieces of their prompts and strategies, which we can learn from to improve guardrails and detection. If we’re lucky, we may even be able to trace the activity back to whoever made the requests. However, if they fine-tune an open-weight model on their own hardware, they’ll easily be able to hide/destroy the evidence after carrying out the attack. This means we not only can’t track the attacker, but we might also never find out how the attack succeeded, which leaves us vulnerable to similar attacks in the future.

One could argue that open-weight models have weaker cyber capabilities, which is a hard technical limitation for attackers. There are two counterarguments here. First, open-weight models aren’t that far behind and the gap in cyber capabilities has been closing quite quickly, so there really isn’t that drastic of a difference, especially since attackers don’t always target the most complex vulnerabilities. Second, I imagine a majority of the most persistent and well-coordinated attacks (which are the ones we should be most worried about) are backed by people with enough technical background and determination to carry them out anyway—sure, using open-weight models instead of frontier models might make the attack slower or more of a hassle, but I’m not sure it’s the dealbreaker here. Does that few-month advantage in cybersecurity capabilities really justify locking up frontier models, if it means pushing people towards open-weight models and all the consequences that come with them?

Moreover, there are longstanding black markets for zero days, vulnerability exploits, etc. I expect this to continue into the age of AI: attackers can use these underground platforms to coordinate, find ways around the regulations, share malicious models and attack strategies, and more. They will play dirty and stop at nothing to get frontier access or as close to frontier access as possible (for example, the API access situation I mentioned earlier). If we can’t beat them at their illegal game, then we might as well open up the playing field so that our defenders have access to the best resources too. Attackers are still a small portion of the population; we have so much more talent, resources, etc on our side, and providing frontier access would overall be tremendously useful for defense.

Finally, regardless of what policy decisions come next, open-weight models are already out there and there’s no going back. AI seems like it’s on track to become its own category of critical national weapon, similar to traditional CBRNE weapons. We already see major players like Russia, China, Singapore, and the EU starting to use open-weight models to assist and automate various military operations. Unfortunately, these models also tend to have worse cybersecurity/safety training and can probably be hacked or fine-tuned much more easily than frontier models. This is an extremely dangerous combination that we now have to live with, and I hope it will become a high-priority topic of international discussion going forward.
